Sure!  In programming, a loop is a series of instructions that repeat, so we can do more than one step of code in the same way.  For example, you can repeatedly print out the numbers 0 to 10:

for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    cout << i << endl;
}

A loop is typically used with instructions like printf("%d", i); that would print the numbers 0 through 9.  You can often build loops out of the commands you’re already using.  In other words, you can do something like this:

printf("%d", i);

and make i 0, 1, 2, 3, ... until you get to 9.  Then you can do a loop, like this:

for (i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
    cout << "The value of i is " << i << endl;
}

